        <register>
          <name>PIC_VECTOR</name>
          <description>Interrupt vector base address. DS §4.7.6.4 p.174. Source x is delivered to the CPU as vector x + vector0; with the reset vector0 = 32 this gives Table 30's 'default interrupt vector' column (source + 32). The stock firmware's dispatcher indexes its ISR table by (vector - 32) (IRAM 0x1064a; dm32uv-qemu board-model notes (DM32-BOARD-MODEL.md), session 7). Offset first identified from a live register read of a running radio, and also in DRV bsp_vic.h. Reset 0x20 (DS).</description>
          <addressOffset>0xc</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000020</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>vector0</name>
              <description>Vector number for IRQ0, range 32-112. Source x is delivered as vector x + vector0 [reset 0x20] [access RW]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>7</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>PIC_COW1</name>
          <description>End of interruption. DS §4.7.6.5 p.174. The hand-maintained entry this replaced guessed a 'clear-on-write ack'; the datasheet defines it as the end-of-interrupt register (eoi for normal, feoi for fast interrupts). Offset also in DRV bsp_vic.h. FW: no write found by the program-flash sweep, which does not cover the IRAM interrupt entry path. Reset 0x0 (DS).</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>write-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>feoi</name>
              <description>When the CPU finishes processing the fast interrupt, it writes 1 to this bit . After obtaining FEOI , the interrupt controller clears the FISR register and pulls the FINT (active low) request line high. 1 ： CPU interrupt service ends; 0 ： CPU Still processing outage service. [reset 0x0] [access WO]</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>eoi</name>
              <description>When the CPU finishes processing the interrupt, it writes 1 to this bit . After receiving the eoi signal, the interrupt controller will clear the ISR register and pull the INT (active low) request line high. 1 ： CPU interrupt service ends; 0 ： CPU Still processing outage service. [reset 0x0] [access WO]</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>write-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>PIC_PRIOR0_3</name>
          <description>Priority counter initial values, sources 0-3. DS §4.7.6.6 p.174. Sources 0-3 are unassigned, TIMER_1, TIMER_2, TIMER_3; one byte each, source 0 in [7:0]. Offset also in DRV bsp_vic.h as PIC_PRIOR0_3. FW: no access found by the program-flash sweep (which does not cover IRAM code). Reset 0x3020100 (DS).</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x03020100</resetValue>
